#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.11"
# dependencies = [
#     "capabilities-contract>=0.50,<1",
#     "slack_sdk>=3.33",
#     "websocket-client>=1.8",
# ]
# ///
"""
slacker — read and write a Slack workspace as the linked user, over their own session.

This is the menu; `slacker help <verb>` is one verb's whole contract.

Agent-invoked. Links the person's OWN Slack account by its web-client
session (the workspace `xoxc-` token plus the shared `d` cookie), the way the
`whatsapp` and `telegram` capabilities emulate their vendor's own client, and
reads only what that account can already see: direct messages, group direct
messages, and the public and private channels it belongs to, their message
history and thread replies. It keeps that history in the project's Postgres
store, syncs it forward incrementally, and a bundled service captures it live.

It also writes as that person does in their own client: it sends a message to a
DM, group DM or channel, replies in a thread (optionally also to the channel),
edits and deletes the account's own messages, adds and removes reactions,
uploads a file with a comment, marks a conversation read, and opens a DM or group
DM. Each write is refused with exit 4 unless the connection grants writes
(`allow_write`, given per connection), and what it writes lands in the store as
the capture records it. It sets no presence and joins no channel. The session is
the person's own, used transparently as one more of their browser sessions —
there is no concealment, no monitoring bypass, and no client-identity spoofing.
Run `slacker doctor` first to prove the session and the workspace.

Startup protocol
  1. `slacker doctor` — proves the session authorises (`auth.test`), resolves
     the linked user and the workspace, and matches the connection's binding.
  2. Exit 2 means the session is invalid or expired (`invalid_auth`): the person
     links the connection again (its `d` cookie is short-lived; there is no
     silent refresh).
  3. Exit 6 is a configuration fault (no connection, no store) or a write Slack
     refused (not your message, an archived conversation); exit 4 is policy (a
     disabled capability, or a write on a connection that does not grant
     writes); exit 5 is the store or Slack being unreachable.

Connections
A connection is one workspace session of one linked account: the env vars that
hold its two secrets, never their values, plus the non-secret workspace
identity. A machine with several workspaces declares one connection each. Link
one with `slacker link` (a Chrome window) or by paste with `slacker connection
add` (`slacker guide linking`); how a connection is declared, selected and
resolved is on `slacker help connections`.

Store
The capture lives in the project's Postgres database, in slacker_* tables keyed
by the workspace account (the team id), created and migrated on first use. With
no database every verb that reads or writes the capture exits 6; each verb's
page says whether it needs one, and `slacker help migrate` says where it comes
from.

Commands
  slacker link [<id>]                link a workspace by signing in through Chrome
  slacker whoami                     the linked identity and workspace, named
  slacker conversations              the conversations the account can see
  slacker history <channel>          a conversation's messages, from the store or Slack
  slacker thread <channel> <ts>      a thread's replies, from the store or Slack
  slacker search <query>             Slack's own message search, store not needed
  slacker sync                       bring the store forward from Slack

Writes (each needs a connection that grants writes; exit 4 otherwise)
  slacker send <channel> <text>      send a message, or reply in a thread
  slacker edit <channel> <ts> <text> edit one of the account's own messages
  slacker delete <channel> <ts>      delete one of the account's own messages
  slacker react <channel> <ts> <e>   add the account's reaction to a message
  slacker unreact <channel> <ts> <e> remove the account's reaction
  slacker upload <channel> <path>    upload a file, with a comment, to a conversation
  slacker mark <channel> [<ts>]      mark the conversation read
  slacker open <user> [<user> ...]   open a DM or group DM, and answer its channel
Each answers the channel and ts it wrote, and records the write in the store as
the live capture records the same act; a read mark is not captured.

The assistant's prompts and jobs
  slacker prompts list               each prompt and where its text comes from
  slacker prompts show <key>         one prompt as the next turn reads it
  slacker prompts put <key>          a new active version, from stdin
  slacker prompts history <key>      every stored version, newest first
  slacker prompts retire <key>       back to the bundle's standard text
  slacker jobs help                  the jobs verbs, in one list
  slacker jobs list                  jobs in this project, newest first
  slacker jobs active                only what is waiting or running
  slacker jobs show <id>             one job, staged amendments included
  slacker jobs register <outcome>    write a draft; nothing runs it yet
  slacker jobs submit <id>           hand a draft to the runner
  slacker jobs amend <id> <text>     continue a job with added context
  slacker jobs discard <id>          drop a draft nobody will submit
  slacker jobs stop <id>             stop the work; it keeps its session
  slacker jobs resume <id>           continue a stopped job

Service (the bundled listener: live capture, and the assistant that answers
the messages its settings admit; `slacker guide assistant-service`)
  slacker service init               seed the control files and the settings
  slacker service run                run the listener in the foreground
  slacker service reload             make the running listener take the settings
  slacker service status             the last health the listener wrote
  slacker service logs               the tail of the listener's log
  slacker service doctor             store, session, what the settings reach
  slacker service start              deferred to the deployment capability
  slacker service stop               deferred to the deployment capability
  slacker service help               the service verbs, in one list
The assistant's settings are the contract's `slacker settings`, below.

Success is JSON on stdout; structured errors are JSON on stderr. Exit 4 is a
policy refusal.
"""

